# Live E2E credentials — copy to e2e/.env and fill in what you have.
#
# GENERATED by `pnpm --filter @theokit/plugins-e2e env:example`. Edit
# e2e/src/services.ts, not this file.
#
# Nothing runs without E2E_LIVE=1, so a stray `pnpm e2e` cannot spend money
# or send email. Every variable is optional: a service whose credentials are
# absent SKIPS with a named reason instead of failing.

E2E_LIVE=0

# ── Email (Resend) — @theokit/plugin-email (via Resend, api-key)
# ⚠ Sends real email. Resend free tier covers this comfortably, but the mailbox will accumulate messages — the suite does not delete them.
#   Resend API key, `re_…`
#   ↳ resend.com/api-keys → Create API key (sending access is enough)
RESEND_API_KEY=
#   Verified sender address the key is allowed to send as
#   ↳ resend.com/domains → verify a domain, then use any address on it. Resend refuses an unverified from-address, so this cannot be arbitrary.
RESEND_FROM_ADDRESS=
#   Mailbox the test may deliver into
#   ↳ Any mailbox created for this and nothing else. Every message carries a run marker, so search `theokit-e2e` to find what the suite sent.
EMAIL_TEST_RECIPIENT=

# ── Payments (Stripe) — @theokit/plugin-payments (via Stripe, api-key)
# ⚠ Test mode only. The readiness report refuses to treat a key that does not start with sk_test_ as ready, and the sk_test_ prefix is re-asserted on the response of a real call (session ids come back cs_test_…), not just at credential-check time. Sessions are not cleaned up; they expire in 24h. Measured 2026-08-18 and deliberately NOT asserted: GET /v1/events?types[]=… does not validate the event type — a fabricated name returns HTTP 200 with an empty list, exactly like a valid one with no matches — so a check that the provider EVENT_MAP keys are still real Stripe events would pass with an invented name. Stripe publishes no endpoint enumerating valid types; that map is covered by review, not by this suite.
#   Stripe TEST-mode secret key, `sk_test_…`
#   ↳ dashboard.stripe.com/test/apikeys — must be the test key. A live key here would move real money.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
#   ONE-TIME price id in test mode the suite may build a checkout session for
#   ↳ dashboard.stripe.com/test/products → create a product with a one-time price. It must NOT be recurring: StripeProvider hardcodes mode:"payment", and Stripe refuses a recurring price in that mode ("You specified `payment` mode but passed a recurring price"). Point this at a throwaway product — the one in use is named "theokit-e2e — do not use", not anything from a real catalogue.
STRIPE_TEST_PRICE_ID=

# ── Copilot (OpenRouter LLM) — @theokit/plugin-copilot (via OpenRouter, api-key)
# ⚠ Costs money per run, in fractions of a cent. Keep the prompt short.
#   OpenRouter API key, `sk-or-…`
#   ↳ openrouter.ai/keys → Create key, and add a small credit balance
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
#   Model slug to spend on, e.g. `openai/gpt-4o-mini`
#   ↳ openrouter.ai/models — pick a cheap one. The existing in-package probe measured ~$0.000032 per round trip on gpt-4o-mini.
COPILOT_TEST_MODEL=

# ── Voice (OpenAI / Groq) — @theokit/plugin-voice (via OpenAI (TTS + STT), Groq (STT), api-key)
# ⚠ Costs a fraction of a cent per run: one short tts-1 sentence plus one whisper transcription of the ~1s clip it produces. GROQ_API_KEY is optional and only lights up the Groq STT backend; without it that one test skips and the OpenAI path still runs. Note the variable is OPENAI_API_KEY, not OPEN_AI_API_KEY — the plugin itself falls back to the former (DEFAULT_STT_ENV_VAR/DEFAULT_TTS_ENV_VAR), so the other spelling looks configured and silently is not.
#   OpenAI API key, `sk-…` — used for both TTS and STT
#   ↳ platform.openai.com/api-keys
OPENAI_API_KEY=
#   Voice id to synthesize with — one of alloy, echo, fable, onyx, nova, shimmer
#   ↳ Any of the six the plugin accepts (see VALID_VOICES in options.ts)
VOICE_TEST_TTS_VOICE=

# ── Auth (GitHub OAuth) — @theokit/auth-github (via GitHub OAuth, oauth-redirect)
# ⚠ Only the token exchange is reachable unattended. Measured 2026-08-17: unauthenticated, /login/oauth/authorize answers 302 → /login BEFORE validating anything, so a fabricated client_id — even an empty one — gets the same 302 as the real app. Any "GitHub accepted our authorize URL" assertion therefore passes with no credential at all, and redirect_uri mismatch is enforced only after login. Both were written, measured, and deleted.
#   OAuth app client id
#   ↳ github.com/settings/developers → New OAuth App
GH_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
#   OAuth app client secret
#   ↳ Same app → Generate a new client secret
GH_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#   Callback URL registered on the app
#   ↳ Same app → Authorization callback URL. GitHub rejects an exchange whose redirect_uri does not match, which is one of the things worth asserting.
GH_OAUTH_CALLBACK_URL=

# ── Auth (Google OIDC) — @theokit/auth-google (via Google OIDC, oauth-redirect)
# ⚠ Same three-legged limit as GitHub. What IS fully testable here is discovery: the plugin fetches Google’s real OIDC document, and that is a live contract with no human in it.
#   OAuth 2.0 client id
#   ↳ console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials → Create OAuth client ID (Web)
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
#   OAuth 2.0 client secret
#   ↳ Same credential → client secret
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#   Redirect URI registered on the client
#   ↳ Same credential → Authorised redirect URIs
GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=

# Optional: lights up the Groq STT backend in the voice suite.
# ↳ console.groq.com/keys
GROQ_API_KEY=
